The question doesn't name a framework, but the context covers it for Next.js (App Router). There are two options.

## Whole page: `loading.js`

Add a `loading.js` file in the same folder as your `page.js`. Next.js automatically wraps the page in a `<Suspense>` boundary and uses your component as the fallback [1][2].

```tsx
// app/blog/loading.tsx
export default function Loading() {
  // Define the Loading UI here
  return <div>Loading...</div>
}
```

The layout renders immediately, the loading UI shows instantly, and the page's HTML replaces it when the page finishes loading [1]. Put your spinner (or a skeleton) in this component instead of the text [1][2].

## Part of a page: `<Suspense>`

To show a fallback for only part of the page, wrap the component that fetches data in `<Suspense>` with a `fallback`:

```tsx
// app/dashboard/page.tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import { PostFeed, Weather } from './Components'

export default function Posts() {
  return (
    <section>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading feed...</p>}>
        <PostFeed />
      </Suspense>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading weather...</p>}>
        <Weather />
      </Suspense>
    </section>
  )
}
```

The fallback shows while the component's async work is in progress, then the component is swapped in [5].

## Pending state for Server Functions

If you mean a spinner while a Server Function (mutation) runs, use React's `useActionState`, which returns a `pending` boolean [3]:

```tsx
'use client'

import { useActionState, startTransition } from 'react'
import { createPost } from '@/app/actions'
import { LoadingSpinner } from '@/app/ui/loading-spinner'

export function Button() {
  const [state, action, pending] = useActionState(createPost, false)

  return (
    <button onClick={() => startTransition(action)}>
      {pending ? <LoadingSpinner /> : 'Create Post'}
    </button>
  )
}
```

If you're using Angular instead, the docs provided describe an `@loading` block for deferred loading, but that's for `@defer` dependencies, not page data fetching [4].

Use the `useSearchParams` hook from `next/navigation`. It's a Client Component hook that returns a **read-only** version of the [`URLSearchParams`](https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams) interface. [1]

```tsx
'use client'

import { useSearchParams } from 'next/navigation'

export default function SearchBar() {
  const searchParams = useSearchParams()

  const search = searchParams.get('search')

  // URL -> `/dashboard?search=my-project`
  // `search` -> 'my-project'
  return <>Search: {search}</>
}
```
[1]

Useful methods on the returned object:

- `get()` returns the first value for a parameter, or `null` if it's absent. For `/dashboard?a=1&a=2`, `get("a")` returns `'1'`, and you use `getAll()` for all values. [4]
- `has()` returns a boolean indicating whether the parameter exists. [4]

**Client Component pages:** If the Client Component is a page, you can instead read the `searchParams` prop with React's `use` hook, since the prop is a promise. [3][5]

```tsx
'use client'
import { use } from 'react'

export default function Page({
  searchParams,
}: {
  searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>
}) {
  const filters = use(searchParams).filters
}
```
[3]

Note that this `searchParams` prop is a plain JavaScript object, not a `URLSearchParams` instance. [3]

**Layouts:** Layouts don't re-render on navigation, so they can't access search params directly. Read them in a Client Component (using `useSearchParams`) rendered inside the layout, because Client Components re-render on navigation and get the latest values. [2]

Call `redirect()` from `next/navigation` in the Server Component when the user isn't signed in. It throws a `NEXT_REDIRECT` error and stops rendering of that route segment [2].

```tsx
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation'

export default async function Profile({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ id: string }>
}) {
  const { id } = await params
  const team = await fetchTeam(id)

  if (!team) {
    redirect('/login')
  }

  // ...
}
```
[2]

You don't need `return redirect()`, because `redirect` uses the TypeScript `never` type [2].

**Centralizing the check:** The docs recommend a Data Access Layer with a `verifySession()` function that reads the session cookie and calls `redirect('/login')` if there's no `userId`. You can then call it from your data requests, Server Actions, and Route Handlers [4].

```tsx
import 'server-only'

import { cache } from 'react'
import { cookies } from 'next/headers'
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation'
import { decrypt } from '@/app/lib/session'

export const verifySession = cache(async () => {
  const cookie = (await cookies()).get('session')?.value
  const session = await decrypt(cookie)

  if (!session?.userId) {
    redirect('/login')
  }

  return { isAuth: true, userId: session.userId }
})
```
[4]

**Optional Proxy layer:** You can also pre-filter unauthenticated users in Proxy with `NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))`. The docs say to only read the session from the cookie there (optimistic checks) and avoid database checks [1][3].

Wrap a folder name in square brackets to create a dynamic segment. For `/blog/my-first-post`, create `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx`, where `[slug]` is the dynamic segment [1][3].

## Reading the slug

Dynamic segments are passed to the page as the `params` prop. In the examples, `params` is a `Promise`, so you `await` it and destructure `slug` [1]:

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  return <div>My Post: {slug}</div>
}
```
[1]

A request to `/blog/a` gives `params` of `{ slug: 'a' }`, and `/blog/b` gives `{ slug: 'b' }` [1].

## Using the slug to fetch data

You can use the value to load content for that page [3]:

```tsx
export default async function BlogPostPage({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const post = await getPost(slug)

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p>{post.content}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```
[3]

## Other places `params` is available

Dynamic segments are also passed as `params` to `layout`, `route`, and `generateMetadata` functions [1].

## Other segment forms

Use `[...segment]` for catch-all and `[[...segment]]` for optional catch-all routes [2].

Yes. Export `generateStaticParams` from the dynamic route segment and return the full list of params, so every path is rendered statically at build time [1][3]:

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://.../posts').then((res) => res.json())

  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }))
}
```
[1]

Next.js then generates multiple versions of the page using the returned `params` [3]. Requests made with `fetch` inside `generateStaticParams` are automatically deduplicated, which speeds up the build [5].

If you use Cache Components, the route is executed with each sample param during the build to collect the HTML. If dynamic content or runtime data is accessed incorrectly, the build fails [2].

Prerendering every page isn't always ideal. Each prerendered page adds build work and output that has to be stored and deployed, and many routes may never be visited before your next deployment [4]. To prerender only a subset, return a partial list and the rest are rendered on first visit [1].

Create a Route Handler with the `route.ts` (or `route.js`) file convention inside the `app/` directory. Route Handlers use the Web `Request` and `Response` APIs. [3][4]

## Basic GET endpoint

```ts
// app/api/route.ts
export async function GET() {
  return Response.json({ message: 'Hello World' })
}
```
[4]

A `GET` export in `/app/api/route.ts` handles `GET` requests sent to `/api`. [3]

## Using `NextResponse.json()`

`NextResponse.json()` also produces a response with a JSON body, and lets you set options such as the status:

```ts
// app/api/route.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'

export async function GET(request: Request) {
  return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Internal Server Error' }, { status: 500 })
}
```
[1]

## Error handling

Use `try/catch` for operations that may throw, and return an appropriate status:

```ts
// app/api/route.ts
import { submit } from '@/lib/submit'

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  try {
    await submit(request)
    return new Response(null, { status: 204 })
  } catch (reason) {
    const message =
      reason instanceof Error ? reason.message : 'Unexpected error'

    return new Response(message, { status: 500 })
  }
}
```
[3]

## Things to keep in mind

- Route Handlers are public HTTP endpoints that any client can access. To restrict access, implement authentication and authorization. [3]
- Avoid exposing sensitive information in error messages sent to the client. [3]
- For a dynamic route, you can export `generateStaticParams` alongside `GET` to statically generate API responses at build time. [5]
- A new project created with `create-next-app --api` includes an example `route.ts` in the `app/` folder. [2]

The docs cover this for Next.js and Angular, so both are below.

## Next.js

**Route segment level:** add an `error.tsx` file inside the segment. It wraps that segment and its nested children in an error boundary and shows the fallback UI when something throws. It must be a Client Component. [1][5]

```tsx
'use client' // Error boundaries must be Client Components

import { useEffect } from 'react'

export default function ErrorPage({
  error,
  retry,
}: {
  error: unknown
  retry: () => void
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.error(error)
  }, [error])

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```
[1][5]

Errors bubble up to the nearest parent error boundary, so you can place `error.tsx` files at different levels of the route hierarchy. [1] `error.js` doesn't wrap the `layout.js` or `template.js` in the same segment. For the root layout, use `global-error.js`. [5]

**Component level:** use `catchError` to create a boundary you can wrap around any part of the component tree. [1][4]

```tsx
'use client'

import { catchError, type ErrorInfo } from 'next/error'

function ErrorFallback(props: { title: string }, { error, retry }: ErrorInfo) {
  const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{props.title}</h2>
      <p>{message}</p>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}

export default catchError(ErrorFallback)
```
[4]

Then use it as a wrapper:

```tsx
import ErrorBoundary from './custom-error-boundary'

export default function Component({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <ErrorBoundary title="Dashboard Error">{children}</ErrorBoundary>
}
```
[3]

**Caveat:** error boundaries don't catch errors in event handlers or other async code. Catch those yourself and store them with `useState` or `useReducer`, then render a fallback. [3] Unhandled errors inside `startTransition` do bubble up to the nearest error boundary. [3]

## Angular

Wrap the risky part of the template in a `@boundary` block with an `@error` block. If a component or directive inside throws during initialization or change detection, the `@error` content renders instead. [2]

```angular-html
@boundary {
  <app-risky-component />
} @error {
  <p>Something went wrong!</p>
}
```
[2]

The docs provided don't cover React Native or Jest for this question.

Put it in a `proxy.ts` (or `.js`) file in the project root, or inside `src` if you use one, at the same level as `pages` or `app`. [2] Proxy runs on the server before a request is completed and before routes are rendered. [2] Starting with Next.js 16, this was called Middleware, and the `middleware` file convention is deprecated in favor of `proxy`. [2][3]

Here's an auth redirect example, scoped to many routes with `matcher`:

```ts
// proxy.ts
import { NextResponse, NextRequest } from 'next/server'
import { authenticate } from 'auth-provider'

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const isAuthenticated = authenticate(request)

  // If the user is authenticated, continue as normal
  if (isAuthenticated) {
    return NextResponse.next()
  }

  // Redirect to login page if not authenticated
  return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))
}

export const config = {
  matcher: '/dashboard/:path*',
}
```
[1]

Things to keep in mind:

- If you've customized `pageExtensions` (e.g. to `.page.ts`), name the file `proxy.page.ts` or `proxy.page.js`. [2]
- Proxy runs after `redirects` in `next.config.js` and before rendering. [1] For simple redirects, try the `redirects` config in `next.config.ts` first, and use Proxy when you need request data or more complex logic. [3]
- Proxy isn't meant for slow data fetching, and it shouldn't be your full session management or authorization solution. It's fine for optimistic checks like permission-based redirects. [3]
- Don't rely on shared modules or globals in Proxy. To pass information to your app, use headers, cookies, rewrites, redirects, or the URL. [2]

Yes, when you use `next/jest`. It loads `.env` files (and all variants) into `process.env` [2][3]. To enable this, pass your app's path via the `dir` option so `next.config.js` and `.env` files are loaded in the test environment [3]:

```ts
import type { Config } from 'jest'
import nextJest from 'next/jest.js'

const createJestConfig = nextJest({
  dir: './',
})

const config: Config = {
  coverageProvider: 'v8',
  testEnvironment: 'jsdom',
}

export default createJestConfig(config)
```

**Test-specific behavior:**
- Tests run with `NODE_ENV=test`, so Next.js loads `.env.test` and `.env`, but not `.env.development` or `.env.production` [1].
- `.env.local` is not loaded in the `test` environment, so tests produce the same results for everyone [1][4].
- Lookup order is `process.env`, `.env.test.local`, `.env.test`, then `.env` [4].

**Loading env vars without `next/jest`:** if you need to load them yourself, for example in a Jest global setup file, use `loadEnvConfig` from `@next/env` [1]:

```js
import { loadEnvConfig } from '@next/env'

export default async () => {
  const projectDir = process.cwd()
  loadEnvConfig(projectDir)
}
```

The docs also note that to test environment variables directly, you should load them manually in a separate setup script or in your `jest.config.ts` file [3].

Create the mock with `jest.fn()`, then assert on it with `.toHaveBeenCalledWith` or by inspecting its `.mock` property.

## Using `toHaveBeenCalledWith`

`.toHaveBeenCalledWith` checks that the mock was called with specific arguments, using the same algorithm as `.toEqual` [1].

```js
test('registration applies correctly to orange La Croix', () => {
  const beverage = new LaCroix('orange');
  register(beverage);
  const f = jest.fn();
  applyToAll(f);
  expect(f).toHaveBeenCalledWith(beverage);
});
```
[1]

Related matchers:

```js
// Called at least once
expect(mockFunc).toHaveBeenCalled();

// Called at least once with the specified args
expect(mockFunc).toHaveBeenCalledWith(arg1, arg2);

// The last call used the specified args
expect(mockFunc).toHaveBeenLastCalledWith(arg1, arg2);
```
[2]

## Inspecting `.mock.calls` manually

Every mock has a `.mock` property that records how it was called [3]. `mock.calls` is an array with one entry per call, and each entry is the array of arguments for that call [4].

```js
import {forEach} from './forEach';

const mockCallback = jest.fn(x => 42 + x);

test('forEach mock function', () => {
  forEach([0, 1], mockCallback);

  // Called twice
  expect(mockCallback.mock.calls).toHaveLength(2);

  // First argument of the first call was 0
  expect(mockCallback.mock.calls[0][0]).toBe(0);

  // First argument of the second call was 1
  expect(mockCallback.mock.calls[1][0]).toBe(1);

  // Return value of the first call was 42
  expect(mockCallback.mock.results[0].value).toBe(42);
});
```
[5]

The manual equivalents of the matchers are:

```js
// Called with the specified args
expect(mockFunc.mock.calls).toContainEqual([arg1, arg2]);

// Last call used the specified args
expect(mockFunc.mock.calls[mockFunc.mock.calls.length - 1]).toEqual([arg1, arg2]);
```
[2]

Use the manual form when you need a more specific assertion, such as checking only the first argument of the last call [2]. `mock.lastCall` is also available for that: `expect(someMockFunction.mock.lastCall[0]).toBe('test')` [3].

Call `jest.mock('axios')` to automock the module, then set return values on its functions with `mockResolvedValue`:

```js
import axios from 'axios';
import Users from './users';

jest.mock('axios');

test('should fetch users', () => {
  const users = [{name: 'Bob'}];
  const resp = {data: users};
  axios.get.mockResolvedValue(resp);

  // or: axios.get.mockImplementation(() => Promise.resolve(resp))

  return Users.all().then(data => expect(data).toEqual(users));
});
```
[1]

`jest.mock(moduleName)` replaces the module with an auto-mocked version, so auto-mocked functions return `undefined` until you give them a value [2].

**Other options**

- **Custom factory:** pass a second argument to run your own implementation instead of automocking [2]:

  ```js
  jest.mock('../moduleName', () => {
    return jest.fn(() => 42);
  });
  ```

- **ES6 modules with a default export:** when using a factory, set `__esModule: true` manually [2]:

  ```js
  jest.mock('../moduleName', () => {
    return {
      __esModule: true,
      default: jest.fn(() => 42),
      foo: jest.fn(() => 43),
    };
  });
  ```

- **Mock differently within the same file:** use `jest.doMock()`, which isn't hoisted by `babel-jest` like `jest.mock` is [5].

**Caveats**

- A module mocked with `jest.mock` is mocked only in the file that calls it. Other files importing it get the original implementation [3].
- Importing a module in a setup file (`setupFilesAfterEnv`) prevents mocking of that module and everything it imports [3].

There are several equivalent ways to test a promise-returning function in Jest. In all of them, you must return or `await` the promise, or the test will finish before it settles.

## Return the promise

Jest waits for a returned promise to resolve, and the test fails if it rejects. [1]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', () => {
  return fetchData().then(data => {
    expect(data).toBe('peanut butter');
  });
});
```
[1]

## async/await

Put `async` before the test function and `await` the call. [2]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', async () => {
  const data = await fetchData();
  expect(data).toBe('peanut butter');
});
```
[2]

## `.resolves` / `.rejects`

You can use these matchers with `async`/`await`: [2]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', async () => {
  await expect(fetchData()).resolves.toBe('peanut butter');
});

test('the fetch fails with an error', async () => {
  await expect(fetchData()).rejects.toMatch('error');
});
```
[2]

Or return the assertion without `async`: [4]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', () => {
  return expect(fetchData()).resolves.toBe('peanut butter');
});
```
[4]

## Testing rejections with `.catch`

If you expect a rejection and use `.catch`, add `expect.assertions`. Otherwise a fulfilled promise would not fail the test. [2]

```js
test('the fetch fails with an error', () => {
  expect.assertions(1);
  return fetchData().catch(error => expect(error).toMatch('error'));
});
```
[2]

None of these styles is better than the others, and you can mix them. Choose whichever makes your tests simpler. [4]

Use `beforeEach`. It runs a function before each test in the file, which suits resetting global state shared by many tests [1].

```js
const globalDatabase = makeGlobalDatabase();

beforeEach(() => {
  // Clears the database and adds some testing data.
  return globalDatabase.clear().then(() => {
    return globalDatabase.insert({testData: 'foo'});
  });
});
```
[1]

Things to know:

- **Async setup:** If the function returns a promise or is a generator, Jest waits for it to resolve before running the test [1]. Returning the promise works, as in `return initializeCityDatabase();` [4]. Alternatively, the hook can take a `done` parameter [4].
- **Timeout:** You can pass a second argument, `timeout`, in milliseconds. The default is 5 seconds [1].
- **Scoping:** Inside a `describe` block, `beforeEach` runs only for the tests in that block [1].
- **Cleanup:** To clean up after each test, pair it with `afterEach` [3][4].
- **One-time setup:** If you only need setup once before any tests run, use `beforeAll` instead [1][2].

If the state you want to reset is mock state, the `--resetMocks` CLI option does this automatically before every test. It is equivalent to calling `jest.resetAllMocks()` before each test, and it removes mocks' fake implementations without restoring their initial ones [5].

Enable coverage collection either from the CLI or in your config.

**CLI:** run Jest with the `--coverage` flag (alias `--collectCoverage`). [3]

**Config:** set `collectCoverage` to `true` (default is `false`). Note that this adds coverage statements to all executed files, so it may significantly slow down your tests. [5]

```js
// jest.config.js
const {defineConfig} = require('jest');

module.exports = defineConfig({
  collectCoverage: true,
});
```

## Useful related options

- **See a summary in the console:** `coverageReporters` defaults to `["clover", "json", "lcov", "text"]`. If you override it, add `"text"` or `"text-summary"` to get console output. You can pass options in tuple form, e.g. `['text', {skipFull: true}]` to hide fully covered files. [1]
- **Include untested files:** use `collectCoverageFrom` with glob patterns to collect coverage even for files that no test requires. This option requires `collectCoverage: true` or `--coverage`. Patterns are applied in order, so negations like `!**/node_modules/**` must come after the patterns they should override. [2]

```js
const {defineConfig} = require('jest');

module.exports = defineConfig({
  collectCoverage: true,
  collectCoverageFrom: ['**/*.{js,jsx}', '!**/node_modules/**', '!**/vendor/**'],
  coverageReporters: ['clover', 'json', 'lcov', ['text', {skipFull: true}]],
});
```

[1][2][5]

- **Coverage of test-containing source files:** test files are normally ignored for coverage; use `forceCoverageMatch` to include them. [4]

If you see "Coverage data for global was not found" with `Unknown%`, your glob patterns most likely aren't matching any files. [2]

Yes. Jest supports TypeScript in two ways, and you also need to decide how to type the Jest globals.

## Running TypeScript tests

**Via Babel:** Follow the Babel setup first, then install `@babel/preset-typescript` and add it to your presets in `babel.config.js`: [1]

```bash
npm install --save-dev @babel/preset-typescript
```

```javascript
module.exports = {
  presets: [
    ['@babel/preset-env', {targets: {node: 'current'}}],
    '@babel/preset-typescript',
  ],
};
```

Babel only transpiles, so Jest won't type-check your tests as they run. If you want type-checking, use `ts-jest` instead, or run `tsc` separately (or as part of your build). [1]

**Via `ts-jest`:** This is a TypeScript preprocessor with source map support. Install it with: [1]

```bash
npm install --save-dev ts-jest
```

You may also need to create a configuration file for it. [1]

## Typing the Jest globals

There are two options:

1. **`@jest/globals`**: Its type definitions ship with Jest and update whenever you update Jest. Install it and import the APIs you need: [1]

   ```ts
   import {describe, expect, test} from '@jest/globals';
   import {sum} from './sum';

   describe('sum module', () => {
     test('adds 1 + 2 to equal 3', () => {
       expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3);
     });
   });
   ```
   [2]

2. **`@types/jest`**: It provides types for the globals without imports. It's a third-party package maintained at DefinitelyTyped, so the latest Jest features may not be covered yet. Match your Jest and `@types/jest` versions as closely as possible (e.g. Jest `27.4.0` with `@types/jest` `27.4.x`). [2]

The TypeScript examples in the Jest docs (Globals, the Jest object, Mock Functions) only work as documented if you explicitly import the Jest APIs from `@jest/globals`. [3][4][5]

Use `jest.spyOn(object, methodName)`. It tracks calls to `object[methodName]` and, by default, still calls the original method, so the behavior isn't replaced. [1]

```js
const video = require('./video');

afterEach(() => {
  // restore the spy created with spyOn
  jest.restoreAllMocks();
});

test('plays video', () => {
  const spy = jest.spyOn(video, 'play');
  const isPlaying = video.play();

  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalled();
  expect(isPlaying).toBe(true);
});
```
[1]

The original `play()` still runs, so `isPlaying` is `true`. [1]

Some related points:

- Call `jest.restoreAllMocks()` in an `afterEach` hook to restore the original state. [1]
- To overwrite the implementation instead, use `jest.spyOn(object, methodName).mockImplementation(() => customImplementation)`. [1]
- To spy on a getter or setter, pass `'get'` or `'set'` as the third argument (Jest 22.1.0+). [3]
- If your codebase transpiles explicit resource management, `using spy = jest.spyOn(...)` restores the spy automatically when the block exits. [1]

Jest treats ES6 classes as constructor functions with syntactic sugar, so a mock for a class must be a function or an actual ES6 class. You can build it with mock functions [2]. The docs describe several approaches; the ones covered in the provided context are below.

## 1. Automatic mock

If you don't need to replace the implementation, call `jest.mock()` on the module path. The class becomes a mock constructor [1].

```javascript
import SoundPlayer from './sound-player';
import SoundPlayerConsumer from './sound-player-consumer';
jest.mock('./sound-player'); // SoundPlayer is now a mock constructor

beforeEach(() => {
  SoundPlayer.mockClear();
});

it('We can check if the consumer called a method on the class instance', () => {
  const soundPlayerConsumer = new SoundPlayerConsumer();
  soundPlayerConsumer.playSomethingCool();

  // mock.instances is available with automatic mocks:
  const mockSoundPlayerInstance = SoundPlayer.mock.instances[0];
  const mockPlaySoundFile = mockSoundPlayerInstance.playSoundFile;
  expect(mockPlaySoundFile).toHaveBeenCalledWith('song.mp3');
});
```

`SoundPlayer.mockClear()` clears all instances and calls to the constructor and all methods [1].

## 2. Manual mock

Save a mock implementation in a `__mocks__` folder. This lets you specify the implementation and reuse it across test files [5].

```javascript
// __mocks__/sound-player.js
export const mockPlaySoundFile = jest.fn();
const mock = jest.fn().mockImplementation(() => {
  return {playSoundFile: mockPlaySoundFile};
});

export default mock;
```

Then, in the test, call `jest.mock('./sound-player')` and import the mock and the shared mock method [5]:

```javascript
import SoundPlayer, {mockPlaySoundFile} from './sound-player';
import SoundPlayerConsumer from './sound-player-consumer';
jest.mock('./sound-player');
```

## 3. Module factory parameter

`jest.mock(path, moduleFactory)` takes a function that returns the mock. To mock a constructor, the factory must return a constructor function (a function that returns a function) [4].

```javascript
import SoundPlayer from './sound-player';
const mockPlaySoundFile = jest.fn();
jest.mock('./sound-player', () => {
  return jest.fn().mockImplementation(() => {
    return {playSoundFile: mockPlaySoundFile};
  });
});
```

**Caveats:**
- `jest.mock()` calls are hoisted to the top of the file, so Jest blocks access to out-of-scope variables in the factory. The check is disabled for variables whose names start with `mock`, but you must make sure they're initialized in time (watch for the Temporal Dead Zone) [4].
- Using a name like `fakePlaySoundFile` fails with an out-of-scope error [4].
- Defining `const mockSoundPlayer = jest.fn().mockImplementation(...)` outside and returning it directly from the factory throws a `ReferenceError`, because it isn't wrapped in an arrow function and is accessed before initialization after hoisting [4].

A complete test using the factory approach clears both the constructor mock and the method mock in `beforeEach` [3]:

```javascript
beforeEach(() => {
  SoundPlayer.mockClear();
  mockPlaySoundFile.mockClear();
});
```

