Use `Linking.openURL()` from React Native. For web URLs, the protocol (`http://` or `https://`) must be included. [1]

```tsx
import {Linking} from 'react-native';

await Linking.openURL('https://google.com');
```

The method returns a `Promise`. It resolves if the user confirms the open dialog or the URL opens automatically, and rejects if the user cancels or no registered app can handle the URL. [1]

For non-http(s) URLs, it's best to check `canOpenURL()` first. The docs' example does this and shows an alert if the URL isn't supported: [2]

```tsx
import {useCallback} from 'react';
import {Alert, Button, Linking} from 'react-native';

const OpenURLButton = ({url, children}: {url: string; children: string}) => {
  const handlePress = useCallback(async () => {
    const supported = await Linking.canOpenURL(url);

    if (supported) {
      // If the scheme is "http", the link is opened by a browser on the device
      await Linking.openURL(url);
    } else {
      Alert.alert(`Don't know how to open this URL: ${url}`);
    }
  }, [url]);

  return <Button title={children} onPress={handlePress} />;
};
```

**Android note:** to handle `https` schemes with `canOpenURL()`, add this to your manifest: [4]

```xml
<manifest ...>
  <queries>
    <intent>
      <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
      <data android:scheme="https"/>
    </intent>
  </queries>
</manifest>
```

