## React Native

Create an `Animated.Value` starting at 0, bind it to `opacity` on an `Animated.View`, and run `Animated.timing` to animate it to 1 [1][2]:

```tsx
import {useEffect, useRef} from 'react';
import {Animated} from 'react-native';

const FadeInView = props => {
  const fadeAnim = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current; // Initial opacity: 0

  useEffect(() => {
    Animated.timing(fadeAnim, {
      toValue: 1,
      duration: 10000,
      useNativeDriver: true,
    }).start();
  }, [fadeAnim]);

  return (
    <Animated.View style={{...props.style, opacity: fadeAnim}}>
      {props.children}
    </Animated.View>
  );
};
```

On mount, opacity is 0, then the timing animation updates the bound opacity on each frame until it reaches 1 [1].

The `Animated` docs example creates the value with `useAnimatedValue(0)` instead of `useRef`. It also triggers `Animated.timing` from button presses, so you can fade both in and out [3].

