Use an Angular HTTP interceptor. It runs in the injection context of the injector that registered it, so it can use `inject` to get a service like `AuthService` and add the token header to each outgoing request [2].

```ts
export function authInterceptor(req: HttpRequest<unknown>, next: HttpHandlerFn) {
  // Inject the current `AuthService` and use it to get an authentication token:
  const authToken = inject(AuthService).getAuthToken();

  // Clone the request to add the authentication header.
  const newReq = req.clone({
    headers: req.headers.append('X-Authentication-Token', authToken),
  });
  return next(newReq);
}
```
[2]

The request is cloned rather than mutated, and the clone is passed to `next` [1][2]. `HttpHeaders` instances are immutable, so `append()` returns a new instance [5].

To test it, register the interceptor with `withInterceptors` in `TestBed`, and use `HttpTestingController` to check that the request carries the header [1]:

```ts
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  providers: [
    AuthService,
    // Testing one interceptor at a time is recommended.
    provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([authInterceptor])),
    provideHttpClientTesting(),
  ],
});
```
[1]

The docs also show a class-based version (`@Injectable() class AuthInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor`) that does the same thing [1].

The provided docs don't show how to register the interceptor in your real application's providers outside of the test setup, so I can't cover that part.

Add `provideHttpClient` to the `providers` array in `app.config.ts`:

```ts
export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [provideHttpClient(/* add features here, such as withInterceptors(...) */)],
};
```
[1]

Then inject `HttpClient` wherever you need it:

```ts
@Service()
export class ConfigService {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  // This service can now make HTTP requests via `this.http`.
}
```
[1]

**Optional features:** `provideHttpClient` accepts feature configurations such as `withInterceptors(...)` to register interceptor functions, and `withXhr()` to switch from the default `fetch` backend to `XMLHttpRequest` [3]. Don't use `withXhr()` in SSR environments. XHR support on the server is deprecated, and the docs recommend the default `fetch` backend for SSR [3].

**Note:** In Angular v21 and later, `HttpClient` is available for injection by default [2]. The docs provided don't say whether `provideHttpClient` is still needed in that case, for example when you want to add features.

Use Angular's `@for` block. It loops through a collection and renders its content once per item. The collection can be any JavaScript iterable, with extra performance optimizations for arrays [1].

```angular-html
@for (item of items; track item.id) {
  {{ item.name }}
}
```
[1]

A fuller example inside a list:

```angular-html
<ul class="user-badge-list">
  @for (badge of badges(); track badge.id) {
    <li class="user-badge">{{ badge.name }}</li>
  }
</ul>
```
[2]

**`track` expression**
- `track` is part of the syntax. It lets Angular associate your data with the DOM nodes it creates, so it can do the minimum DOM work when data changes [1][2].
- Prefer a unique property such as `id` or `uuid` [1].
- For static collections that never change, you can use `$index` [1].
- Tracking by the item itself (`track item`) uses `===` reference identity. It's a last resort because it can make updates significantly slower [1].

**Fallback for empty lists**

Add an `@empty` block right after the `@for` content. It renders when there are no items [5]:

```angular-html
@for (item of items; track item.name) {
  <li>{{ item.name }}</li>
} @empty {
  <li>There are no items.</li>
}
```

**Contextual variables**

Inside `@for`, `$count`, `$index`, `$first`, `$last`, `$even`, and `$odd` are available. You can alias them with `let` [4]:

```angular-html
@for (item of items; track item.id; let idx = $index, e = $even) {
  <p>Item #{{ idx }}: {{ item.name }}</p>
}
```

**Limitation:** `@for` doesn't support `continue` or `break` [1].

Yes. Use the `viewport` trigger on a `@defer` block. It loads the deferred content when the watched content enters the viewport, using the Intersection Observer API. [2]

```angular-html
@defer (on viewport) {
  <large-cmp />
