By default, the `@defer` watches the `@placeholder` content, which must have a single root element. [2]

**Watch a different element:** pass a template reference variable from the same template as the trigger parameter. [2]

```angular-html
<div #greeting>Hello!</div>
@defer (on viewport(greeting)) {
  <greetings-cmp />
}
```

**Customize the observer:** pass an object literal with `IntersectionObserver` options (everything except `root`). When using this notation with an explicit element, pass it via the `trigger` property. [2]

```angular-html
<div #greeting>Hello!</div>

<!-- With options and a trigger -->
@defer (on viewport({trigger: greeting, rootMargin: '100px', threshold: 0.5})) {
  <greetings-cmp />
}

<!-- With options and an implied trigger -->
@defer (on viewport({rootMargin: '100px', threshold: 0.5})) {
  <greetings-cmp />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Implied trigger</div>
}
```
[5]

**With incremental hydration (SSR):** use `hydrate on viewport` to load dependencies and hydrate the content when it enters the viewport. [1]

```angular-html
@defer (hydrate on viewport) {
  <large-cmp />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Large component placeholder</div>
}
```
[1]

Angular offers two ways to declare a required input, depending on which input API you use.

**Signal-based inputs (recommended for new projects):** call `input.required` instead of `input`.

```ts
@Component(/* ... */)
export class CustomSlider {
  // Declare a required input named value. Returns an `InputSignal<number>`.
  value = input.required<number>();
}
```
[1]

Required inputs don't automatically include `undefined` in the generic parameter of the returned `InputSignal`. [1]

**Decorator-based inputs:** set the `required` option on `@Input`.

```ts
@Component(/* ... */)
export class CustomSlider {
  @Input({required: true}) value = 0;
}
```
[2]

The decorator-based `@Input` API remains fully supported, though the Angular team recommends the signal-based `input` function for new projects. [2]

**Enforcement:** with either approach, if you use the component in a template without specifying all of its required inputs, Angular reports an error at build time. [1][2]

In Angular, a child component raises a custom event with an **output**, and the parent listens to it in its template.

**1. Define the output in the child** by assigning a property with the `output` function:

```ts
@Component({
  /*...*/
})
export class ExpandablePanel {
  panelClosed = output<void>();
}
```

The `output` function returns an `OutputEmitterRef`. You can call `output` only in component and directive property initializers. [2]

**2. Emit the event** by calling `emit` on it, optionally with data:

```ts
this.panelClosed.emit();
```
[2]

```ts
// You can emit primitive values.
this.valueChanged.emit(7);

// You can emit custom event objects
this.thumbDropped.emit({
  pointerX: 123,
  pointerY: 456,
});
```
[1]

**3. Listen in the parent's template.** Use the event binding syntax, and read any event data from `$event`:

```angular-html
<expandable-panel (panelClosed)="savePanelState()" />
```
[2]

```angular-html
<custom-slider (valueChanged)="logValue($event)" />
```
[1]

**4. Receive the data in the parent component:**

```ts
@Component({
 /*...*/
})
export class App {
  logValue(value: number) {
    ...
  }
}
```
[1]

Things to keep in mind:
- Angular custom events do not bubble up the DOM. [2]
- Output names are case-sensitive. [2]
- Outputs are inherited by a child class when you extend a component class. [2]

Decorate the service class with `@Service`. It makes the service a root-level singleton, available throughout the application. [2]

```ts
import {Service} from '@angular/core';

@Service()
export class AnalyticsLogger {
  trackEvent(category: string, value: string) {
    console.log('Analytics event logged:', {category, value});
  }
}
```
[2]

Root-level provisioning gives you three guarantees: a single shared instance for the whole app, global availability without manual provider registration, and tree-shakability (the service is dropped from the production bundle if your code never uses it). [3]

**Alternatives and caveats**

- `@Service` is shorthand for `@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})`, which gives the same singleton behavior. [2][3]
- `@Injectable` requires `{providedIn: 'root'}` explicitly, but it supports constructor-based DI, advanced provider keys like `useClass`, and non-root scopes such as `platform`. `@Service` does not. [3]
- You can also register a provider in `bootstrapApplication`'s `providers` array for a global singleton. This is useful for providers with side-effects, configuration, or the `provideSomething` pattern. Those providers are always included in the bundle, even if never injected. [1]

**Avoid listing the service in a component's `providers` array.** That creates a new instance per component instance, and it's only available to that component and its children. [4][5]

**1. Enable zoneless change detection**

- In Angular v21+, zoneless is the default, so there's nothing to enable. Just make sure `provideZoneChangeDetection` isn't used anywhere to override the default. [2]
- In Angular v20, add `provideZonelessChangeDetection()` at bootstrap: [2]

```ts
bootstrapApplication(MyApp, {providers: [provideZonelessChangeDetection()]});
```

Or with NgModule:

```ts
platformBrowser().bootstrapModule(AppModule);

@NgModule({
  providers: [provideZonelessChangeDetection()],
})
export class AppModule {}
```
[2]

**2. Remove ZoneJS from the build**

- Remove `zone.js` and `zone.js/testing` from the `polyfills` option in `angular.json`, in both the `build` and `test` targets. [1]
- If you use an explicit `polyfills.ts`, remove `import 'zone.js';` and `import 'zone.js/testing';` from it. [1]
- Then uninstall the package: [1]

```shell
npm uninstall zone.js
```

**3. Make your code zoneless-compatible**

- Angular decides when to run change detection from these notifications: `ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck` (called automatically by `AsyncPipe`), `ComponentRef.setInput`, updating a signal read in a template, bound host or template listener callbacks, and attaching a view marked dirty by one of these. [5]
- `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` isn't required, but it's recommended as a step toward compatibility. Components using `Default` work as long as they notify Angular via `markForCheck`, signals, `AsyncPipe`, etc. [5]
- Remove uses of `NgZone.onMicrotaskEmpty`, `NgZone.onUnstable`, and `NgZone.onStable`, since they never emit in zoneless mode. `NgZone.isStable` is always `true`. Use `afterNextRender` or `afterEveryRender` instead. [4]
- `NgZone.run` and `NgZone.runOutsideAngular` can stay. [4]
- With SSR, tell Angular about async tasks that should block serialization using the `PendingTasks` service (or `pendingUntilEvent` for observables). [3]

```typescript
const taskService = inject(PendingTasks);
taskService.run(async () => {
  const someResult = await doSomeWorkThatNeedsToBeRendered();
  this.someState.set(someResult);
});
```
[3]

Hydration works only for server-side rendered (SSR) apps, so enable SSR first by following the Angular SSR Guide. [1]

## Using Angular CLI

If you enabled SSR through the CLI (during app creation or later with `ng add @angular/ssr`), the code that enables hydration should already be in your app. [1]

## Manual setup

For a custom setup, import `provideClientHydration` from `@angular/platform-browser` and add it to your bootstrapping providers. [1]

Standalone bootstrap:

```typescript
import {
  bootstrapApplication,
  provideClientHydration,
} from '@angular/platform-browser';

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideClientHydration()]
});
```
[1]

With NgModules, add it to the root module's providers:

```typescript
import {provideClientHydration} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import {NgModule} from '@angular/core';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [App],
  exports: [App],
  bootstrap: [App],
  providers: [provideClientHydration()],
})
export class AppModule {}
```
[1]

## Include it on the server too

The `provideClientHydration()` call must also be in the providers used to bootstrap the app on the **server**. In projects with the default structure from `ng new`, adding it to the root `AppModule` is enough, since the server module imports that module. With a custom setup, add it to the providers list in the server bootstrap configuration. [1]

If it's missing on the server, you'll hit the NG0505 error ("No hydration info in server response"). [5]

## Incremental hydration

Calling `provideClientHydration()` also enables incremental hydration by default. [2] To opt out, pass `withNoIncrementalHydration()`:

```ts
provideClientHydration(withNoIncrementalHydration())
```
[2]
